{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And I will pass through the land of Egypt.</b> The entire passage is Moses and Aaron's narration to all the congregation of the children of Israel, and therefore Scripture should properly have said 'and YHWH passed through the land of Egypt.' Therefore the rabbis, of blessed memory, make a Midrash on this, saying: 'ועברתי בארץ מצרים' — 'I and not an angel,' that is, not by means of an angel sent from Him with plagues, as in the case of David and Sennacherib. <br><b>And I will smite every firstborn,</b> 'I and not a seraph' — its meaning is that the smiting too will be done by the Holy One, blessed be He, Himself, not like a king who takes vengeance on his enemies before him through his speculator — those being the seraphim from whom issues fire that burns his enemies, as in the case of Elijah and the captains of fifty.<br> <b>And against all the gods of Egypt I will execute judgments,</b> 'I and not the messenger' — the one sent from Him for all the deeds done on earth, which is the great angel called on that account Metatron, for the meaning of the word indicates 'the one who shows the way,' and likewise in Greek the messenger is called mentator.<br><b>I am YHWH.</b> 'I am He and no other' — He is One, and there is no Elohim with Him to stay His hand; thus the Ramban wrote. And the clarification of his words is that had the plague of the firstborn been carried out by His emissaries and great ministers, the smiting would have been in the measure of strict justice, drawn out even against Israel, for they were not worthy of salvation. Therefore the judgment was carried out by the Holy One, blessed be He, Himself and in His glory — that is, the Shekhinah, which is comprised of both the measure of judgment and the measure of mercy, so that Israel would be saved. And this is the language of the Sages: 'and not by the messenger,' meaning not by him alone, but the Holy One, blessed be He, Himself and in His glory. And from this you will find in the plague of the firstborn the expression 'I' (אנכי), which is an allusion to the Shekhinah, as it is written: ״הנה אנכי הורג את בנך בכורך״ — as in the expression used for Jacob, upon whom the Shekhinah descended with him (Genesis 46:4): ״אנכי ארד עמך מצרימה ואנכי אעלך גם עלה״, and this is what is written (ibid. 15): ״וגם את הגוי אשר יעבודו דן אנכי״ — for the judgment was through the Shekhinah, to kill the Egyptians and to save Israel; this is what is written: ״בנגפו את מצרים ואת בתינו הציל״." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'And against all the gods of Egypt I will execute judgments' — those of wood rotted, and those of metal melted. And in the account written in the section 'And YHWH smote all the firstborn,' Scripture there mentions the plague of the firstborn but does not mention these judgments that were done upon their gods, for at the death of the firstborn they were terrified on that night, and the judgments done upon their gods were not known until the next day, when they came to their houses of abomination and saw judgments in them — as in the matter written concerning the gods of the Philistines when they detained the Ark among them (I Samuel 5:3): ״וישכימו האשדודים ממחרת והנה דגון נופל לפניו ארצה״, and it is written: ״וראש דגון ושתי כפות ידיו כרותות אל המפתן״, and then they knew that their smiting and the smiting of their idols was from Heaven. And this is what the prophet said (Jeremiah 10:11): ״כדנה תאמרון להון אלהיא די שמיא וארקא לא עבדו יאבדו מן ארעא ומן תחות שמיא אלה״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The phrase 'כלשון האמור ביעקב' and the second verse cited (Genesis 46:15) refer to the divine 'אנכי' used in the context of judgment upon the nation Israel would serve; rendered as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:58:15.413880670Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}